ight on offer."},{"title":"What it does instead","text":"It threatens …Nxc4, winning a pawn."},{"title":"If White takes the knight","text":"18.Rxd4 is met by 18…Nf3+. Then 19…Nxd4 takes the rook on d4, and 20…Nxb3 takes the bishop on b3. Black ends up two pawns ahead: Black is winning (−4.25)."},{"title":"White's best try","text":"White's best is to leave the knight alone and play 18.c3. Black answers 18…Nxb3, taking the bishop on b3 and threatening …Nxa1, winning a rook. Then 19…Rfd8 threatens …Rxd1+, winning a rook, and 20…Rxd1+ gives check. Material is level and Black holds the balance (−0.37)."}]},{"ply":113,"name":"57.Rh4","cls":"mistake","color":"w","headline":"57.Rh4 is a mistake: White's winning chances go from 28% to 16%.","points":["Black answers 57…Ra2: the rook reaches the seventh rank, where 2 pawns sit.","After Ra2, Black wins 1 point of material within 4 moves.","Better was 57.Kd2 (−2.59)."],"better":"57.Kd2 prepares Rb3 and cxd4+. Engine line: 57…d4 58.Rb3 Ra4 59.cxd4+ Rxd4+ 60.Ke2 (−2.59)."},{"ply":114,"name":"57…Ra2","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"57…Ra2! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["The rook reaches the seventh rank, where 2 pawns sit.","Attacks the b2 pawn, which is undefended.","The next best move, 57…Kd6, would drop Black's winning chances from 84% to 74% (−2.82).","57…Ra2 wins a pawn, picking it up with 58…Rxb2.","Sets up a sequence and wins a pawn once the captures settle, picking it up with 58…Rxb2.","White answers 58.Ke3."]}],"opening":{"name":"Modern Defense","eco":"B06","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 5…O-O: 10 moves in all, 5 by White and 5 by Black.","White left the book with 6.e5. In 100 games from this position, strong players played 6.O-O (76%), 6.Bb3 (11%) or 6.Qe2 (9%).","The main line goes on 6.O-O Nxe4 7.Nxe4 d5 8.Bd3 dxe4 9.Bxe4 Nd7 10.c3 c5.","From here White scored 47% in those games, with 7% drawn."]}}